course: myths
lesson: 8
title: Wild animals and tall tales
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Picture it: an ostrich, terrified, plunges its head into the sand and stays there, bottom in the air, thinking nobody can see it. It is a brilliant picture, and the Romans were already telling it two thousand years ago.
explain: Wild-animal tall tales spread because we see animals from far away, in cartoons, or in old films, and a good story beats a dull truth: lemmings leaping off cliffs, bats that are blind, camels with water tanks on their backs.
explain: Take each one apart and it's even better: the ostrich is turning its eggs in a nest dug in the ground, and the lemming film was staged, with lemmings pushed off a ledge for the camera.
explain: Some tales are nearly true, like the owl that turns its head "all the way round"; it turns about three-quarters of the way, and that is amazing enough.
explain: And some tales are worse than wrong: the claim that sharks never get cancer was used to sell useless pills, and many sharks were killed for them.
fact: Ostriches don't bury their heads; when in danger they lie flat or run at up to about 70 kilometres an hour, and they turn their eggs in nests dug in the ground.
fact: Lemmings don't kill themselves in groups; a 1958 Disney film staged it, and when there are too many lemmings they spread out and some drown crossing water by accident.
fact: There is no good evidence that elephants fear mice; like anyone, they may startle at a sudden small movement near their feet.
fact: All bats can see, and fruit bats rely on their eyes; many bats also find their way by echolocation, listening to echoes of their own calls.
fact: Porcupines can't shoot their quills; the quills come loose easily when touched and stick into an attacker.
fact: A camel's hump stores fat, not water; camels save water very cleverly and can drink a huge amount at once.
fact: Owls can turn their heads about 270 degrees, three-quarters of a circle, and special blood vessels let them do it safely.
fact: Chameleons change colour mostly to show mood, send signals and control their temperature, not to match whatever they sit on.
fact: Sharks do get cancer, and pills made of shark cartilage (the bendy stuff a shark's skeleton is made of) don't cure it; the myth was used to sell pills and has harmed sharks.
fact: Wolves howl to talk to their pack and mark their territory; the Moon doesn't make them howl.
fact: A tortoise's shell is part of its skeleton, joined to its backbone and ribs, so it can never climb out of it.
fact: Even a documentary can be staged, so it is worth asking how a scene was filmed.
exercise: The quiz says ostriches bury their heads in the sand when scared. What do they really do in danger? | Lie flat or run very fast | Bury their heads until it's gone | Hide inside their eggs | Climb the nearest tree
exercise: Why might an ostrich look as if its head is buried, from far away? | It is turning its eggs in a nest dug in the ground | It is drinking water that is hidden deep in the sand | It is sleeping with its head tucked under the ground | It is listening for worms moving about under the sand
exercise: Why do many people believe lemmings jump off cliffs together? | A 1958 film staged it by pushing lemmings off a ledge | Scientists filmed it happening every spring | Lemmings are known to love jumping | It is written in every encyclopaedia as true
exercise: What do lemmings really do when there are too many of them? | Spread out to new places, and some drown crossing water by accident | Hold a big meeting on the cliff top and then all jump into the sea together | Hibernate until there are fewer | Turn into a different kind of animal
exercise: Ryan says elephants are scared of mice. What's true? | There's no good evidence; they may just startle at surprises | It's proven that elephants run away from every mouse they ever see | Elephants are scared of all grey animals | Mice can climb up elephants' trunks
exercise: Holly says "blind as a bat". What's true about bats? | All bats can see, and many also use echolocation | Bats have no eyes at all, only ears on their heads | Bats can only see during the daytime, never at night | Bats find their way only by smelling things around them
exercise: Will says porcupines shoot their quills at you. What really happens? | Quills come loose easily when touched | They fire quills like arrows across a room | They throw quills with their front paws | They spit quills from their mouths
exercise: Martha says camels store water in their humps. What's in a hump? | Fat | Water | Milk | Air
exercise: How do camels survive so long without water? | Their bodies waste very little water and they can drink a lot at once | They suck water from their humps | They drink sand that holds water | They never need any water at all
exercise: A zoo visitor says owls turn their heads all the way round. How far can they really turn? | About 270 degrees, three-quarters of the way | A full 360 degrees, and then a little bit more | Only about 90 degrees, just like we can turn ours | Not at all, so they have to turn their whole body
exercise: Jack's comic says chameleons change colour to match what they sit on. Why do they mostly change? | To show mood, send signals and control temperature | To match whatever tartan or polka dots they are sitting on | Because they eat coloured food | Only when they fall asleep
exercise: A pills advert says sharks never get cancer, so shark cartilage stops it. What's true? | Sharks do get cancer and the pills don't cure it | Sharks never get ill at all | The pills cure cancer in people | Only great white sharks are protected
exercise: Who was hurt by the shark cartilage myth, as well as buyers? | Sharks, killed for their cartilage | Nobody at all, because it was only an advert | Whales, which people had mistaken for sharks | Tortoises, whose shells were used to make the pills
exercise: Grandpa says wolves howl at the Moon. Why do wolves howl? | To call their pack and mark their territory | Because the bright moonlight hurts their sensitive eyes | To make the Moon come out | Only when they are hungry for cheese
exercise: Daisy's cartoon tortoise steps out of its shell. Why is that impossible? | The shell is part of its skeleton, joined to spine and ribs | The shell is glued on by its mother | The tortoise is too shy to leave it | The shell would shrink without the tortoise
exercise: What is echolocation? | Finding your way by listening to echoes of your own calls | Seeing in the dark with glowing eyes | Smelling food through the air | Feeling the wind with your whiskers
test: Your mate swears: "I SAW it on a nature film, lemmings jumped off a cliff, so it's true." What do you say? | That film was staged; real lemmings don't do that | If something is shown on film, then it must always be true | They only jump on Tuesdays | Lemmings jump because they can fly a bit
test: Asking "is a tortoise leaving its shell like me leaving my skeleton?" Why is that a good comparison? | Because the shell really is its ribs and backbone | Because people could also step out of their skeletons | Because tortoises have no skeleton at all inside them | Because tortoise shells are made of a kind of human bone
test: "Lol blind as a bat, my brother can't find his socks." Is the saying good science? | No, bats can see; it's just a saying | Yes, bats are totally blind | Yes, bats see nothing but socks | No, bats see better than eagles in daylight
test: In a cartoon, a camel's hump goes flat and water pours out. What's wrong? | Humps hold fat, not water | Nothing, humps are water bottles | The water should be fizzy | Camels keep water in their ears
test: What would happen if you touched a porcupine's back? | Quills could come loose and stick into your hand | It would fire its sharp quills at you from far away | Nothing, quills are soft like fur | It would roll away like a ball
test: Your friend says, "Owls can spin their heads in a full circle, like a cartoon." Wise reply? | "Nearly! About three-quarters round, still amazing." | "Totally, they can turn their heads two full times round." | "No, owls can't turn their heads at all." | "Only baby owls can do that."
test: A shop sells "shark cartilage, because sharks never get cancer". What two questions should you ask? | Is it true, and who makes money from saying it? | What colour is the box, and is it big? | Is the shark famous, and how old was it when it was caught? | Is it tasty, and can I have two?
test: A chameleon on a green leaf turns bright red when another male comes near. Why? | It's signalling to the other chameleon | It's trying hard to match the colour of the leaf | It has just eaten a bright red berry from the bush | It's much too cold for it to stay green
test: A picture shows a wolf howling under a full moon. What's the likely truth? | Wolves howl to their pack; the Moon just makes a good picture | The full moon makes every wolf in the forest howl at it all night long | Wolves only ever howl at the Moon | The wolf is calling the Moon down
test: A huge elephant suddenly steps back when a leaf blows across its feet. What does that suggest? | Elephants can startle at sudden small movements, not mice in particular | Elephants are terrified only of mice, because mice can climb up their trunks | The leaf was a hidden mouse | Elephants can't see their feet at all
test: Why do tall tales about wild animals spread so easily? | We see wild animals from far away or in films, and a good story beats a dull truth | Scientists invent them to test children | Animals behave differently on different days | Old books are always accurate
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct of the ostrich when threatened? | It lies flat upon the ground or flees at considerable speed | It conceals its head beneath the sand | It retreats into a burrow beneath its nest | It feigns death by burying its whole body
exam: Identify the substance principally stored in the hump of a camel. | Fat | Water | Blood | Air
exam: It is given that a widely viewed documentary depicted lemmings leaping from a cliff in large numbers. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The scene was staged by the film-makers, the lemmings having been forced | The film recorded a regular annual event in which lemmings jump from cliffs | The lemmings were attempting to reach the sea to feed | The scene depicted a species other than lemmings
exam: Ascertain the maximum rotation of the head of which an owl is capable. | Approximately two hundred and seventy degrees | A complete rotation of three hundred and sixty degrees | Approximately ninety degrees | Two complete rotations
